<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awadh</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Oudh</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Awadh, or Oudh, is a region of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uttar-pradesh">Uttar Pradesh</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges</a> and the Himalayan foothills. From the 1720s it was ruled by the Nawabs of Awadh, first as Mughal governors and then as near-independent rulers, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faizabad">Faizabad</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucknow">Lucknow</a>. The British annexed it in 1856, a grievance that fed the rebellion of 1857, and joined it to the United Provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awadh.jpg" alt="Awadh" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: रूही · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sarayu_River_night_view,_Ayodhya_001.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Aurangzeb’s death in 1707, a rapid succession of weak rulers failed to hold the vast empire together, and provincial governors in Hyderabad, Bengal and <b>Awadh</b> established effectively autonomous states.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Conversion lightened the tax under Firuz Shah, while a village that resisted or failed to pay could be enslaved: Balban had ordered it in <b>Awadh</b>, and Alauddin Khalji made it lawful for revenue defaulters.</blockquote><a href="/societies/delhi-sultanate">Social Hierarchy of the Delhi Sultanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Maratha Empire defeated the combined army of the Nizam of Hyderabad and several Mughal generals at Bhopal, a coalition that also took in the Rajputs of Jaipur and the Nawabs of <b>Awadh</b> and Bengal.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-24">24 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1722 the Mughal emperor Muhammad Shah appointed Saadat Ali Khan, a Shia noble from Nishapur, as governor of <b>Awadh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/awadh-state">Awadh State</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought the Marathas and the Awadh State; Ahmad Khan Bangash defeated an <b>Awadh</b> army in 1750 but was then crushed by a Maratha and Awadh alliance.</blockquote><a href="/state/bangash-nawabs-of-farrukhabad">Bangash Nawabs of Farrukhabad</a></li><li><blockquote>Mansa Ram, a Bhumihar landholder, obtained the zamindari of Benares around 1737 under the Nawab of <b>Awadh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/benares-state">Benares State</a></li></ul></section>
